const intro={"n":15,"title":"Old Vizcacha’s Advice: Survival, Judges, Cunning and Self-Interest","nums":[757,758,767],"summary":"The younger son recalls the advice given by his guardian Old Vizcacha, often while drinking. The sayings urge him to protect his own skin, court the favor of a judge, avoid drawing attention and choose advantageous company. Vizcacha also speaks distrustfully about women and recommends carrying weapons. These are Vizcacha's words as remembered by the boy, not moral instructions endorsed by the poem or by its author.","people":"Martín Fierro's younger son as narrator; Viejo Vizcacha as the speaker of the sayings. Fierro himself is not the speaker here.","themes":"Survival and self-interest; cynical relations with authority; proverbs and folk wisdom; prudence versus opportunism; misogyny; ethical contrast with Fierro’s later advice.","words":"pellejo (skin, figuratively one's life); palenque (horse-tethering post, figuratively support); pulpero (rural trader); facón (large knife); cueva (den or refuge).","questions":["Who reports Vizcacha's sayings, and under what circumstances?","How does advice about befriending a judge reflect unequal social power?","What distinction can you draw between reasonable caution and exploiting other people?","How do Vizcacha's self-interested maxims compare with Fierro's counsel about brothers and loyalty?","What prejudices appear in Vizcacha's views of women?"],"analysis":"The ethical contrast is not absolute, and isolated proverbs should be read with their speaker and scene. Vizcacha treats authority, danger and relationships as problems of personal advantage. Compare these exact stanzas with Canto XXXII, where Fierro urges solidarity, while remembering that Fierro too is a complicated narrator. The phrases belong to distinct characters, never to an undifferentiated 'José Hernández'.","related":"/en/library/old-vizcacha-vs-martin-fierro/","relatedTitle":"Compare Old Vizcacha and Martín Fierro's advice"};
